In a fixed-layout table, Ladybird now scales column percentages that add
up to more than 100% down in proportion, and still clamps them in order
in auto mode. Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 give the same
widths in all nine cases of the new test, run with engine-compare: with
a length column, an auto column, col elements, cell padding, and with
percentages that add up to exactly 100%.

Blink’s ApplyCellConstraintsToColumnConstraints() multiplies each
column’s percent by 100 / total when the table is in fixed layout and
the total is over 100. WebKit’s FixedTableLayout::layout() scales the
percent columns to the table width when the widths add up to more, and
Gecko’s FixedTableLayoutStrategy::ComputeColumnISizes() reduces the
percentage columns by one ratio when they take up too much space. This
paragraph comes from reading their sources, not running them.
